A program naming no platform package reaches raw elements only by inlining the
stdlib wrappers it did use, which happens after the check. The elements still
have to render: vbox comes out as a styled div and text as a span, with no
element declaration named anywhere in the source.

The other direction -- `sngl:platform/html` imported and its elements written
by hand, with a boolean attribute bound to state -- is
html_element_decl_reactive.txtar.
-- stdlib.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output { none { html() } }

window("t") {
    vbox {
        text(value="hello")
    }
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
<!-- Code generated by sngl (html) from stdlib.sngl; DO NOT EDIT. -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>t</title>
  <style>
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head><body>

<div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column">
  <span>hello</span>
</div>

<script>
(() => {
  // sngl-entry.js
  var state = { visible__inst0: true };
})();
</script>
</body></html>
